<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><language>en</language><title>News from Europe - Latest world headlines and daily news - France 24</title><description>FRANCE 24 explores events across Europe, including in the 27 member states of the European Union and the United Kingdom. Follow our on-the-ground reports, interviews and analyses of the events affecting social and political life on the Continent and Europe’s role in the world.</description><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/</link><lastBuildDate>Wed, 04 Nov 2026 15:06:40 GMT</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.france24.com/en/europe/rss" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><category></category><title>Ukrainian's 'not expecting drastic change in US policy' after midterms</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/ukrainian-s-not-expecting-drastic-change-in-us-policy-after-midterms</link><description>
Ukrainians do not expect the Democrats' victory in the US midterm elections is change much in regards to US policy on the war with Russia, or at least not quickly, says France 24's correspondent in Kyiv Emmanuelle Chaze. Ukraine's President Volodymyr Zelensky has not reacted yet to the "blue wave" taking control of Congress in the US. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/6f8dbdd8-d846-11f1-9528-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-cf6b8ece.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/6f8dbdd8-d846-11f1-9528-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-cf6b8ece.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">56045abc-d845-11f1-ae49-af9928dbefd5</guid><pubDate>Wed, 04 Nov 2026 13:26:11 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Russian households face increasing financial strain as war in Ukraine drags on</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261103-russian-households-face-increasing-financial-strain-as-war-in-ukraine-drags-on</link><description>
As Moscow continues to launch strikes across Ukraine, Russian citizens are facing increasing financial strain. Since the start of the war in 2022, utility bills in Russia have risen by more than 50% – over 13% this year alone. These rising costs come as the country grapples with ongoing drone attacks originating from Ukraine.
 

</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/aee025ec-d7a7-11f1-8d0d-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-69532ec1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/aee025ec-d7a7-11f1-8d0d-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-69532ec1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">98ad3528-d7a5-11f1-8de4-c9cb8c061303</guid><pubDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 17:05:54 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Ukraine and Russia are taking the war underground as drones saturate the battlefield</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261103-ukraine-and-russia-are-taking-the-war-underground-as-drones-saturate-the-battlefield</link><description>
Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky last week heralded the success of Operation Veles, during which Kyiv sent troops deep into a disused gas pipeline in the Kupiansk region to confront Russian troops. These underground tactics, which Russian forces have been using for more than two years to slip past Ukrainian lines, are giving Kyiv a new strategy on the battlefield.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4d3ed6c0-d6db-11f1-8e9a-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/pipeline-ukr.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4d3ed6c0-d6db-11f1-8e9a-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/pipeline-ukr.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">107853ac-d79d-11f1-9fc1-79c330b0f657</guid><pubDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 16:52:13 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4d3ed6c0-d6db-11f1-8e9a-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/pipeline-ukr.jpg">© France Médias Monde Graphic Studio</source><dc:creator>Sébastian SEIBT</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Bomb threats against European airlines force Warsaw airport closure, affect several flights</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261103-bomb-threats-disrupt-europe-flights</link><description>
Bomb threats targeted at least 10 European airlines on Tuesday, forcing the temporary closure of a Warsaw airport and affecting at least four Ryanair flights. No explosives were found after the planes all took off and landed safely in different European cities. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/14446db8-d79d-11f1-832b-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/c1595558cdf9bd510c168986d079acd3ba123d74.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/14446db8-d79d-11f1-832b-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/c1595558cdf9bd510c168986d079acd3ba123d74.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">154652ee-d79d-11f1-92b0-79c330b0f657</guid><pubDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 15:41:48 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/14446db8-d79d-11f1-832b-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/c1595558cdf9bd510c168986d079acd3ba123d74.jpg">© Attila Kisbenedek, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>King Charles says he has greater understanding of 'horrors' of slavery, falls short of apology</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261103-king-charles-says-he-has-greater-understanding-of-horrors-of-slavery-falls-short-of-apology</link><description>
King Charles III told the heads of 56 Commonwealth countries on Tuesday that he has a greater understanding of the horrors of slavery, calling it a "shameful chapter of history". This comes as Prime Minister Andy Burnham said he was open to the United Kingdom apologising for the transatlantic slave trade. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7a616320-d799-11f1-b243-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26307507185418.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7a616320-d799-11f1-b243-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26307507185418.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">7d5f02c8-d797-11f1-b6e5-4f05fbfd3658</guid><pubDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 15:26:37 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7a616320-d799-11f1-b243-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26307507185418.jpg">© Matias Delacroix, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Poland foils attempted 'provocation' on Russian border, PM Tusk says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261103-poland-prevented-attempted-provocation-russian-border-pm-tusk-says</link><description>
Prime Minister Donald Tusk said Tuesday that Poland prevented an attempted "provocation" on its border with the Russian exclave of Kaliningrad overnight. He gave few details but said Polish security forces prevented any incident from happening.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f1af66d0-d789-11f1-91be-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/AP23108425555797.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f1af66d0-d789-11f1-91be-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/AP23108425555797.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">9ab2666c-d788-11f1-bfba-b907054521ed</guid><pubDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 13:39:14 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f1af66d0-d789-11f1-91be-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/AP23108425555797.jpg">© Michal Kosc, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>French schools blockade resume on smaller scale as holidays end</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261103-french-schools-blockade-resume-on-smaller-scale-as-holidays-end</link><description>
Students blockaded dozens of high schools across France on Tuesday to maintain pressure on the government over education reforms as trade unions prepared for a nationwide strike later this week. FRANCE 24's Claire Paccalin reports from the ground.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/6dc43350-d775-11f1-9032-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-5c543cf4.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/6dc43350-d775-11f1-9032-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-5c543cf4.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">7a867bc2-d773-11f1-b073-e95e14706df6</guid><pubDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 12:01:02 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Press Review</category><title>Cheese theft becomes rife among Europe's gangsters</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/press-review/20261103-cheese-theft-becomes-rife-among-europe-s-gangsters</link><description>
PRESS REVIEW – Tuesday, November 3: We look at reactions and analyses in Poland, where pro-European Prime Minister Donald Tusk is involved in a standoff with the nationalist president over the composition of the constitutional court. In other news, there's fresh outrage at big tech, after Google went rogue in Finland and cut down 250,000 trees for data centre projects. Finally, cheese is France's most stolen food product, and it's the stuff of cold, calculated crime.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e5d94cc0-d763-11f1-80b8-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-4a7026d2.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e5d94cc0-d763-11f1-80b8-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-4a7026d2.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3e6bb3ca-d762-11f1-8ab4-cbed8dcebb0b</guid><pubDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 10:53:18 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Antonia KERRIGAN</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>European force to hold first Ukraine support exercise in Poland</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261103-european-force-to-hold-first-ukraine-support-exercise-in-poland</link><description>
Around 2,800 troops from 10 European countries will take part in a military exercise in southeastern Poland from November 9 to 22, testing a multinational force designed to support Ukraine after a potential ceasefire. Led by France and Britain, the force aims to bolster Ukraine's security without taking on an offensive role.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3c45eeb4-d724-11f1-9971-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26258377621895.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3c45eeb4-d724-11f1-9971-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26258377621895.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e7960aa8-d727-11f1-94d9-975020f22ee2</guid><pubDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 01:43:00 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3c45eeb4-d724-11f1-9971-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26258377621895.jpg">© Philippe Magoni, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Europe's Gen Z protesters unite over living costs, housing and employment uncertainty</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261103-europe-s-gen-z-protesters-unite-over-living-costs-housing-and-uncertainty-of-getting-jobs</link><description>
Although French high schoolers may have taken the lead in the youth protests that have since spread to Belgium, Germany, Italy, Portugal and Spain, they acted like "a catalyst" for their shared concerns over their future, researchers say, pointing to higher living costs, unaffordable housing and the uncertainty of their employment prospects.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ce39d81c-d73d-11f1-95d9-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/2db172386e31a2bf2fb7a5deb6903704e8b25997.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ce39d81c-d73d-11f1-95d9-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/2db172386e31a2bf2fb7a5deb6903704e8b25997.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f0d8ce50-d724-11f1-bfc0-7fd9425f4201</guid><pubDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 01:21:48 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ce39d81c-d73d-11f1-95d9-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/2db172386e31a2bf2fb7a5deb6903704e8b25997.jpg">© Kirill Kudryavtsev, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Russian strikes kill at least 26 in Ukraine after war's longest ceasefire ends</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261103-russian-strikes-kill-26-in-ukraine-after-longest-ceasefire-ends</link><description>
Russia killed at least 26 people in strikes on a village in southern Ukraine on Monday, including civilians collecting humanitarian aid, emergency workers and police officers. The attack came hours after a rare ceasefire ended, extinguishing hopes of progress towards peace.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/77d61602-d701-11f1-8594-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/c83c0482d49f8575fc0b65f32d551aa79b4d413b.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/77d61602-d701-11f1-8594-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/c83c0482d49f8575fc0b65f32d551aa79b4d413b.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8385d53c-d724-11f1-b9fb-a5172ee7d599</guid><pubDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 01:18:44 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/77d61602-d701-11f1-8594-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/c83c0482d49f8575fc0b65f32d551aa79b4d413b.jpg">© AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>A propos</category><title>Emergency alerts hacking 'designed to undermine trust in government', expert says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/a-propos/20261102-emergency-alerts-hacking-designed-to-undermine-trust-in-government-expert-says</link><description>
Emergency alert systems in Russia and Ukraine were hit in cyberattacks overnight, with fake warnings of possible nuclear strikes broadcast to users in both countries. The systems have since been brought back under control, though much remains unclear about the attacks, including who was responsible. FRANCE 24's Sharon Gaffney speaks with Dr Melanie Garson, Associate Professor in International Security at University College London. She says that these kinds of attacks "are designed to undermine faith in the government's ability to provide trust".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/50c3a45c-d770-11f1-8436-bf1914cabd52/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-135607c1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/50c3a45c-d770-11f1-8436-bf1914cabd52/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-135607c1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f68d6d32-d70c-11f1-a237-f95335add00f</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 22:30:09 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Sharon GAFFNEY</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Emergency alerts hacking 'designed to undermine trust in government', expert says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/emergency-alerts-hacking-designed-to-undermine-trust-in-government-expert-says</link><description>
Emergency alert systems in Russia and Ukraine were hit in cyberattacks overnight, with fake warnings of possible nuclear strikes broadcast to users in both countries. The systems have since been brought back under control, though much remains unclear about the attacks, including who was responsible. FRANCE 24's Sharon Gaffney speaks with Dr Melanie Garson, Associate Professor in International Security at University College London. She says that these kinds of attacks "are designed to undermine faith in the government's ability to provide trust".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/50c3a45c-d770-11f1-8436-bf1914cabd52/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-135607c1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/50c3a45c-d770-11f1-8436-bf1914cabd52/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-135607c1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">ef2b778c-d707-11f1-8388-091b6edfc70c</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 22:28:36 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Spotlight</category><title><![CDATA[The Long Hull: Karl Bushby reflects on perseverance & 'best of humanity' on his 28-year journey home]]></title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261102-the-long-hull-karl-bushby-reflects-on-perseverance-best-of-humanity-on-his-28-year-journey-home</link><description>
"They say it takes a village. In this case, it took a world." After 28 years and some 50,000 kilometres, British explorer and adventurer Karl Bushby has returned to his hometown of Hull, completing an extraordinary life journey across four continents that illustrates not only the limits of human endurance, but also the intricate relationship between individual determination, geopolitical constraints and a world of humanity. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/cc84e560-d761-11f1-865c-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-b1612aa0.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/cc84e560-d761-11f1-865c-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-b1612aa0.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">9e81c9d8-d6ff-11f1-9be8-5196f7422720</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 20:54:38 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>François PICARD</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>EU says fridge attacks 'deliberate attempt to disrupt' bloc's food supply</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/eu-says-fridge-attacks-deliberate-attempt-to-disrupt-bloc-s-food-supply</link><description>
Grocery stores from Sweden to Portugal were hit Monday by a suspected cyberattack on their freezer systems, in what the EU denounced as a deliberate attempt to disrupt the bloc's food chain. Supermarkets in Belgium, Estonia, Finland, Ireland, Luxembourg and the Netherlands were among at least eight countries affected in the incident, which came three weeks after a cyberattack caused mass disruption in French grocery stores. Food retailers were forced to throw out produce that had spoiled or was at risk of spoiling after refrigerator temperatures soared overnight. FRANCE 24's Dave Keating reports from Brussels.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bcd91bec-d6fb-11f1-8781-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-211244-211858-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bcd91bec-d6fb-11f1-8781-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-211244-211858-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">c2660d18-d6fb-11f1-a834-a3d33105ee23</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 20:30:07 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Russia again pummels Ukraine after rare pause in fighting in Kherson</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261102-russia-pummels-ukraine-again-after-rare-pause-in-fighting-in-kherson</link><description>
Intense fighting between Russia and Ukraine again picked up after a rare ceasefire allowed for a humanitarian mission to reach the war-torn, Russian-occupied city of Oleshky in the southern Kherson region. While Russia accused Kyiv of sending hundreds of drones toward Moscow overnight into Tuesday, Kyiv said Russia had killed dozens of civilians lining up for aid in Zaporizhzhia. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bd2acd8c-d6ee-11f1-8073-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/00a4fa45277e01eb86e800974ef7e00d7a64002b.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bd2acd8c-d6ee-11f1-8073-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/00a4fa45277e01eb86e800974ef7e00d7a64002b.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">bdcc948c-d6ee-11f1-b2f8-4b7d190d8ef0</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 18:53:49 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bd2acd8c-d6ee-11f1-8073-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/00a4fa45277e01eb86e800974ef7e00d7a64002b.jpg">© Handout, Ukrainian Emergency Service, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Refrigeration cyberattacks highlight risks of connecting industry to the internet</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261102-caught-cold-fridge-cyberattack-highlights-internet-gadget-weaknesses</link><description>
Cyberattacks affecting supermarket refrigeration systems on Monday in several European countries – including Belgium, Estonia, Finland, Ireland, Luxembourg, the Netherlands and Sweden – have highlighted the risks of connecting industrial infrastructure to the internet.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/48dab10e-d6d0-11f1-b62d-bf1914cabd52/w:1024/p:16x9/9d184e67dc748e00594d4b7526509569de9d828e.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/48dab10e-d6d0-11f1-b62d-bf1914cabd52/w:1024/p:16x9/9d184e67dc748e00594d4b7526509569de9d828e.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">0118a4da-d6e3-11f1-ba5e-f187f555e5ea</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 17:29:48 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/48dab10e-d6d0-11f1-b62d-bf1914cabd52/w:1024/p:16x9/9d184e67dc748e00594d4b7526509569de9d828e.jpg">© Fredrik SANDBERG / TT NEWS AGENCY/AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>British adventurer arrives back at mother's home after 28-year journey</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261102-british-adventurer-arrives-back-at-mother-s-home-after-28-year-journey</link><description>
British adventurer Karl Bushby enjoyed an emotional reunion with his mother and family on Monday, as he arrived back home after a 28-year, 50,000-kilometre trek from South America, crossing four continents on foot.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f06a2dc2-d6db-11f1-bc19-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/mini3-2.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f06a2dc2-d6db-11f1-bc19-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/mini3-2.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">130d557a-d6dc-11f1-bcef-5b6dff722978</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 16:45:04 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Ukraine and Russia report fake emergency alerts after cyberattacks target apps</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/ukraine-and-russia-report-fake-emergency-alerts-after-cyberattacks-target-apps</link><description>
Authorities in Ukraine and Russia said their emergency alert mobile apps had been targeted by cyberattacks, with users in Ukraine receiving false warnings of an imminent nuclear threat. Moscow said that hackers had sent fake emergency alerts after gaining access to the Russian Emergency Situations Ministry’s mobile app, and that efforts were ongoing to restore the app's functionality.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/30e69a66-d6cd-11f1-800e-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-471ee61c.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/30e69a66-d6cd-11f1-800e-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-471ee61c.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">9a629f90-d6cc-11f1-b20f-673158319541</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 15:06:41 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Fresh wave of fridge cyberattacks hits European supermarkets</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261102-fresh-wave-of-fridge-cyberattacks-hits-european-supermarkets</link><description>
Stores and supermarkets across Belgium, Luxembourg, Finland, Estonia and Sweden all reported early Monday abrupt temperature spikes in their fridges and freezers in an apparent cyberattack targeting refrigeration units. The incidents follow a similar attack that hit hundreds of supermarkets across France in October, triggering food loss and fires.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/071116b6-d6b0-11f1-99f9-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D64Y2DM.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/071116b6-d6b0-11f1-99f9-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D64Y2DM.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">5631e158-d6bf-11f1-aa7d-61c7f4643f18</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 13:40:09 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/071116b6-d6b0-11f1-99f9-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D64Y2DM.jpg">© Fredrik Sandberg, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Italian journalists under police protection as threats rise across the country</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261102-italian-journalists-under-police-protection-as-threats-rise-across-the-country</link><description>
Italy is the European country with the largest number of journalists under police protection. There, journalism is a profession particularly susceptible to threats and intimidation, but the European Union's measures to protect press freedom are slow to be implemented. FRANCE 24's team has more.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8bb11af2-d6b9-11f1-9e68-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-0bf09737.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8bb11af2-d6b9-11f1-9e68-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-0bf09737.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">112b5a04-d6b9-11f1-8741-83bfd1635af1</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 13:20:55 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Press Review</category><title>'Consolidation of power': Bulgaria elects Iliana Iotova as first woman president</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/press-review/20261102-consolidation-of-power-bulgaria-elects-iliana-iotova-as-first-woman-president</link><description>
PRESS REVIEW – Monday, November 2: Bulgarian papers discuss the country's new president. In Belarus, some political prisoners are held in psychiatric wards instead of prisons. Also: Hitler's Eagle's Nest burned down. Will it be rebuilt and should it? Finally, the queen of Halloween, Heidi Klum, flaunts her most disgusting costume yet. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9b1864be-d6af-11f1-8f3e-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-28bd6ec2.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9b1864be-d6af-11f1-8f3e-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-28bd6ec2.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">0cd346f4-d68e-11f1-8e5b-63690de1f67e</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 11:29:15 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Elitsa GADEVA</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Former UK paratrooper Karl Bushby ends 28-year trek across jungles and frozen sea</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261102-former-uk-paratrooper-karl-bushby-ends-28-year-trek-across-jungles-and-frozen-sea</link><description>
Former British paratrooper Karl Bushby on Monday came to the end of his 28-year trek around the world, arriving in his native city of Hull after having swum across the English Channel. His journey took him from the southern tip of Latin America up across the frozen Bering Strait before crossing through Central Asia and Europe to return home.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/14df6f3c-d6b4-11f1-b571-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/148e4d6e97f932b0c02bc9f3e3fabc4fcd763aaf.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/14df6f3c-d6b4-11f1-b571-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/148e4d6e97f932b0c02bc9f3e3fabc4fcd763aaf.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">187b7068-d6a6-11f1-b054-4f8c44edd8f0</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 10:13:48 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/14df6f3c-d6b4-11f1-b571-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/148e4d6e97f932b0c02bc9f3e3fabc4fcd763aaf.jpg">© Paul Ellis, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>France: Some schools blocked as limited youth protests resume after holidays</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261102-france-some-schools-blocked-as-limited-youth-protests-resume-after-holidays</link><description>
French students returning from autumn holidays blocked some high schools on Monday, November 2, determined to reinvigorate a protest movement despite government concessions during the break. FRANCE 24's Antonia Kerrigan reports from the ground.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a9a22dce-d6a2-11f1-bad3-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-f04d5e3a.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a9a22dce-d6a2-11f1-bad3-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-f04d5e3a.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e618d376-d6a1-11f1-889f-fd311e97047e</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 09:57:47 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>RSF publishes list of press freedom 'predators': 'Dictators want to silence journalists'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/rsf-publishes-list-of-press-freedom-predators-dictators-want-to-silence-journalists</link><description>
Eve Irvine is pleased to welcome Thibaut Bruttin, Director General and Secretary-General of Reporters Without Borders (RSF), who says, “When a journalist is killed, it is everyone's right to know what is being endangered.” His warning captures the central democratic dilemma explored in this interview marking the United Nations' International Day to End Impunity for Crimes Against Journalists. As journalists confront lethal violence, imprisonment, political intimidation and increasingly hostile working environments, the struggle for press freedom has become inseparable from a more fundamental question: who controls the public's access to truth? For RSF, press freedom is not simply a journalist's right to speak, but society's right to know. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/34a73f5a-d698-11f1-a86b-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-081019-081929-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/34a73f5a-d698-11f1-a86b-1beafce15fea/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261102-081019-081929-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3a660b42-d698-11f1-b872-9f3d80b6a168</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 09:06:52 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Russia bombing kills at least 26 people at aid centre in Ukraine's Zaporizhzhia region</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261102-live-russia-and-ukraine-trade-blame-cyber-attacks-fragile-ceasefire-ends</link><description>
Russian forces on Monday bombed ‌a village in Ukraine's southeastern Zaporizhzhia region, killing at least 26 people including two rescue workers, Ukrainian authorities said. Ukraine's President Volodymyr Zelensky alleged that ‌Russian forces repeatedly dropped aerial bombs ‌on a school in the village of Komyshuvakha that was being used to distribute humanitarian assistance. Read our liveblog to see how the day's events unfolded.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3e494fe4-d6e3-11f1-ac21-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-20T090542Z-673408585-RC266OAL7JNA-RTRMADP-3-UKRAINE-CRISIS-EVACUATION-ZAPORIZHZHIA-REGION.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3e494fe4-d6e3-11f1-ac21-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-20T090542Z-673408585-RC266OAL7JNA-RTRMADP-3-UKRAINE-CRISIS-EVACUATION-ZAPORIZHZHIA-REGION.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">372e232e-d684-11f1-bcc0-a99176443041</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 06:19:40 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3e494fe4-d6e3-11f1-ac21-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-20T090542Z-673408585-RC266OAL7JNA-RTRMADP-3-UKRAINE-CRISIS-EVACUATION-ZAPORIZHZHIA-REGION.jpg">© Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Russia and Ukraine accuse each other of hacking emergency alert apps</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261102-ukraine-suspects-nationwide-air-raid-alert-app-has-been-hacked</link><description>
Russia and Ukraine accused each other of hacking their emergency alert applications after a local ceasefire in the war's eastern Kherson region expired on Sunday. The cyberattacks came as both sides warned of renewed fighting and Kyiv signalled it could halt strikes on Russian oil refineries in exchange for US Patriot missiles.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/01e27286-d5fa-11f1-9fb6-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/6a3c233d865056dcf34ec2006538c9fc902893e3.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/01e27286-d5fa-11f1-9fb6-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/6a3c233d865056dcf34ec2006538c9fc902893e3.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">a0ced658-d65b-11f1-ae13-8b30643331f8</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Nov 2026 01:20:44 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/01e27286-d5fa-11f1-9fb6-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/6a3c233d865056dcf34ec2006538c9fc902893e3.jpg">© Roman Pilipey, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Poland rocked by Constitutional showdown</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/poland-rocked-by-constitutional-showdown</link><description>
Poland’s constitutional crisis deepened on Saturday as the European Commission urged President Karol Nawrocki to recognise new judges appointed by Prime Minister Donald Tusk. The standoff comes ahead of next year’s parliamentary election, with polls suggesting a close race between Tusk’s coalition and the nationalist right. Camille Corcoran reports.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a499c734-d5de-11f1-94fb-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261101-110736-110939-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a499c734-d5de-11f1-94fb-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261101-110736-110939-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">a93435c2-d5de-11f1-bba3-75c6e95985cc</guid><pubDate>Sun, 01 Nov 2026 13:41:40 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Ceasefire in Ukraine's Kherson region ends after Russia refuses extension</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261101-localised-ceasefire-in-ukraine-s-kherson-region-set-to-end</link><description>
The longest truce of the war in Ukraine, localised in the region of Kherson, ended on Sunday after Russia refused to extend the ceasefire. The temporary pause in fighting, which began on Tuesday, enabled a humanitarian mission to the Russian-occupied town of Olechky.
